r -> Client: {type: 'error', message: string}\n\nFor the OT approach, I'll implement a simple but correct OT for insert/delete operations.\n\nThe key insight for OT with multiple clients:\n- Each client has a version number (how many operations they've seen)\n- When a client sends an operation at version V, the server transforms it against any operations that have been applied since V\n- The server assigns the next version number\n- Broadcast the operation (already transformed) to all other clients\n\nLet me think about this more carefully:\n\nServer maintains:\n- document content\n- version counter (increments with each applied operation)\n- list of connected clients per document\n\nWhen client sends operation at their_version:\n1. Server transforms the operation against operations from their_version to current_version\n2. Server applies the transformed operation to get new content\n3. Server saves version + operation to DB\n4. Server broadcasts operation to all other clients\n5. Server sends ack to sender with new version\n\nActually, I need to be more careful. The OT approach for multi-client:\n\nEach connected client has a \"last seen version\" (the version of the doc when they last got an update). When a client sends an operation:\n\n1. The server transforms the incoming op against all ops that happened between the client's last version and current version\n2. Apply the transformed op to the document\n3. Increment version\n4. Broadcast to ALL other clients (transformed appropriately for each client's version)\n5. Or more simply: broadcast to all other clients, and they each transform locally\n\nActually, the simplest correct approach:\n\nFor each document:\n- Maintain a list of operations in version order\n- Current content is the result of applying all operations to the initial document state\n\nWhen a client connects:\n- Send them: {type: 'init', content: currentContent, version: currentVersion}\n\nWhen a client sends an operation at their_version:\n1. If the