st. Common approaches:\n- Use client ID as tiebreaker\n- Server always wins (server's op stays, client's op shifts)\n\nI think for simplicity, if positions are equal, the already-applied operation stays, and the incoming one shifts. So in the above: A stays at position 5, B shifts to 11. \"hello world there\" is the result.\n\nBut wait, in the transform function I wrote:\n- If op.pos < against.pos: unchanged\n- Else (op.pos >= against.pos): shift\n\nSo if op.pos == against.pos, shift. This means the \"against\" operation was already applied, and the incoming op is adjusted to apply after it. This seems reasonable.\n\n**Scenario 3: Insert and delete**\n- State: \"hello world\"\n- Client A: delete(5, 6) -> \"hello\"\n- Client B: insert(5, \" beautiful\") -> \"hello beautiful world\"\n- Server gets A first: apply delete(5, 6) -> \"hello\"\n- Transform B against A: insert(5, \" beautiful\") vs delete(5, 6)\n  - insert pos (5) <= delete pos (5), so: unchanged, but...\n  - Wait, in my logic for insert vs delete:\n    - if op.pos <= against.pos: return op unchanged\n    - But op.pos == against.pos == 5, and the delete removes positions 5-10. The insert at position 5 means \"insert before the character at position 5\", which is the ' ' (space). After the delete, that space is gone.\n    - Actually, if we think about it: in the original document, position 5 is the space. Client B inserts \" beautiful\" at position 5, so the result would be \"hello beautiful world\". Client A deletes from position 5 for 6 characters, so \" world\" is deleted, resulting in \"hello\".\n    - If A goes first, we get \"hello\". Then B's insert at position 5... but now position 5 is beyond the string \"hello\" (length 5). This would be append! So B's insert(5, \" beautiful\") becomes at position 5, after \"hello\", giving \"hello beautiful\".\n    - Wait, is this right? In the original string \"hello world\", position 5 is the space. Client B inserts at position 5, so before the space. After deletion of positions 5-10 (the space and \"world\"), the result is \"hello\". Then we need to apply B's insert. Where should it go?\n    \n    The answer depends on the OT transform. Let me trace through:\n    \n    Op B before transform: insert(5, \" beautiful\") — insert at pos 5\n    Against Op A: delete(5, 6) — delete positions 5..10\n    \n    In the transformed insert op:\n    - op.pos (5) <= against.pos (5): so my rule says unchanged. But is that right?\n    - Actually, the rule \"if insert.pos <= delete.pos: unchanged\" means the insert happens before anything deleted. Since position 5 is where the delete starts, the insert at position 5 is inserting before the deleted content. After deletion of \" world\", the content becomes \"hello\". The insert at position 5 now happens at end of \"hello\".\n    \n    So: \"hello\" + \" beautiful\" = \"hello beautiful\" ✓\n    \n    That seems right! The character being inserted was at the boundary of the deleted range, and it ends up at the boundary.\n\n**Scenario 4: Insert inside deleted region**\n- State: \"hello world\"\n- Client A: delete(0, 5) -> \" world\"\n- Client B: insert(2, \"xyz\") -> \"hexyzllo world\"\n- Server gets A first: apply -> \" world\"\n- Transform B against A: insert(2, \"xyz\") vs delete(0, 5)\n  - op.pos (2) > against.pos (0): yes\n  - op.pos (2) < against.pos + against.length (0 + 5 = 5): yes, inside deleted region\n  - So: return insert(against.pos=0, \"xyz\") -> insert(0, \"xyz\")\n  - Apply: \" world\" -> \"xyz world\" \n  - Is this semantically correct?\n  \n  Original: \"hello world\"\n  B inserts at pos 2: \"he\"+\"xyz\"+\"llo world\" = \"hexyzllo world\"  \n  A deletes 0-5: deletes \"hello\" -> \" world\"\n  \n  If A goes first then B: \" world\" with B's transformed insert(0, \"xyz\") = \"xyz world\"\n  \n  Is that the same as B then A?\n  B then A: \"hexyzllo world\" then delete 0-5... but after B's insert, the document changed. A's delete at 0 for 5 chars now deletes \"hex\" + \"yz\" may