parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"94645184-9d61-4647-b304-ae0c7e469be4","uuid":"e24724b8-0d9f-4063-a654-1ee078681fc2","timestamp":"2026-05-30T21:49:08.192Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/tests/server.test.ts","content":"/**\n * Tests for the Real-Time Collaborative Document Editor.\n *\n * Covers:\n *  1. OT engine correctness (apply, transform, compose)\n *  2. Server REST API (CRUD + version history)\n *  3. WebSocket collaboration / convergence\n */\n\nimport { describe, it, expect, beforeEach, afterEach } from 'vitest';\nimport { randomUUID } from 'crypto';\nimport WebSocket from 'ws';\nimport { apply, transform, compose } from '../src/ot.js';\nimport { createApp, type AppHandle } from '../src/server.js';\n\n// ── Helper ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nconst TEST_DB = `test-${randomUUID()}.db`;\n\n/** Start the server on a random port and return the URL. */\nasync function startServer(): Promise<{ handle: AppHandle; port: number; url: string }> {\n  const handle = createApp(TEST_DB);\n  const port = await handle.start(0);\n  const url = `http://localhost:${port}`;\n  return { handle, port, url };\n}\n\n/** Wait for a WebSocket to open. */\nfunction wsConnect(url: string): Promise<WebSocket> {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n    const ws = new WebSocket(url);\n    ws.on('open', () => resolve(ws));\n    ws.on('error', reject);\n  });\n}\n\n/** Read a JSON message from a WebSocket (with timeout). */\nfunction wsRead(ws: WebSocket, timeout = 2000): Promise<unknown> {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n    const timer = setTimeout(() => reject(new Error('WebSocket read timeout')), timeout);\n    ws.once('message', (data) => {\n      clearTimeout(timer);\n      try {\n        resolve(JSON.parse(data.toString()));\n      } catch (e) {\n        reject(e);\n      }\n    });\n    ws.once('error', reject);\n  });","numLines":50,"startLine":1,"totalLines":610}}}
+{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"458c29fe-8830-4380-bb3e-1089cf866876","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The issue is that the message event might arrive before the `ws.once('message', ...)` listener is registered. In the `ws` library, when the connection opens, the server sends the `init` message. This is processed asynchronously. But the `ws.on('open')` fires, then `wsRead` is called, but by the time `wsRead` sets up its `ws.once('message', ...)` listener, the message might have already been sent and the event emitted.\n\nThe fix: I should wait for the message AND the open event together, so I don't miss the message.\n\nLet me rewrite `wsConnect` to return both the WebSocket and the first message:\n\n```javascript\nasync function wsConnectAndInit(url: string, timeout = 2000): Promise<{ ws: WebSocket; init: unknown }> {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n    const ws = new WebSocket(url);\n    const timer = setTimeout(() => reject(new Error('WebSocket connect timeout')), timeout);\n    \n    ws.on('open', () => {\n      // Message handler for first message\n      ws.once('message', (data) => {\n        clearTimeout(timer);\n        try {\n          resolve({ ws, init: JSON.parse(data.toString()) });\n        } catch (e) {\n          reject(e);\n        }\n      });\n    });\n    \n    ws.on('error', (err) => {\n      clearTimeout(timer);\n      reject(err);\n    });\n  });\n}\n```\n\nBut wait, the message might still arrive between `ws.on('open')` callback and `ws.once('message')`. In that case, the race still exists.\n\nActually, in Node.js, WebSocket events are emitted on the next tick or asynchronously. When `ws.on('open')` fires, the WebSocket connection is fully established. The server sends the init message as a WebSocket frame. This frame is read from the socket on the next iteration of the event loop. So by the time `ws.on('open')` callback runs, and we set up `ws.once('message')` synchronously, the message event hasn't been emitted yet. The message event will be emitted later when the frame is processed.\n\nSo setting up `ws.once('message')` inside the `ws.on('open')` callback should work, because:\n1. `open` event fires\n2. In the callback, we set up the `message` listener synchronously\n3. The `message` event is emitted later (on the next tick or when data arrives)\n\nBut wait, the `ws` library might emit the `message` event synchronously with `open`... Let me check by looking at the ws library source.\n\nActually, the `ws` library uses streams internally. The `open` event fires when the TCP connection is established and the HTTP upgrade is complete. The `message` event fires when data (WebSocket frames) arrives on the stream. Since the server sends the init message right after accepting the connection, the data might already be in the stream buffer when `open` fires.\n\nIn Node.js, when a `data` event is emitted on a stream, it's emitted synchronously. But the `ws` library processes frames through its own internal pipeline. Typically:\n1. TCP connection established\n2. HTTP upgrade complete → `open` event on WebSocket\n3. Server sends init message (sent asynchronously on server side)\n4. Client receives the WebSocket frame → `message` event\n\nThe key question: is the `message` event emitted in the same tick as the `open` event?\n\nLooking at the ws library code, the `open` event is emitted when the socket is writable and the upgrade response was received. The `message` event is emitted when a complete frame is received from the receiver. These are separate events on the event loop, so they shouldn't be in the same tick.\n\nBut in my Node.js version, this might still race. Let me just make the test more robust by using a different approach:\n\nInstead of having separate `wsConnect` and `wsRead` calls, combine them to set up the listener before the open event fires.\n\nActually, let me look at this from a different angle. The debug test worked! In the debug test, I had:\n```javascript\nconst ws = new WebSocket(wsUrl);\nws.on('error', ...);\nws.on('open', () => { console.log('WS open'); });\nws.on('message', (data) => { console.log('WS message:', data.toString()); });\nws.on('close', ...);\nawait new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 1000));\n```\n\nAll the listeners are set up BEFORE `open` fires (because listening to events is synchronous). Then the `open` event fires, and then the `message` event fires. Both are caught by the listeners.\n\nIn the main test:\n```javascript\nconst ws = await wsConnect(url); // sets up 'open' listener, awaits it\n// At this point, the WebSocket is open\nconst msg = await wsRead(ws); // sets up 'message' listener\n```\n\nThe issue is the `await` between `wsConnect` and `wsRead`. If the message arrives between these two awaits, `wsRead` won't capture it.\n\nBut in Node.js, when you `await`, you yield to the event loop. So there's a window where:\n1. `wsConnect` resolves (open event fired)\n2. Event loop runs other callbacks\n3. Server's init message arrives (message event fired, but nobody is listening)\n4. `wsRead` sets up the listener (too late!)\n\nSo the fix is to combine connect and read into one operation:\n\n```javascript\nfunction wsConnectAndRead(url: string, timeout = 2000): Promise<{ ws: WebSocket; msg: unknown }> {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n    const ws = new WebSocket(url);\n    const timer = setTimeout(() => reject(new Error('Timeout')), timeout);\n    \n    ws.on('open', () => {\n      // Set up message listener immediately, synchronously in the open callback\n      ws.once('message', (data) => {\n        clearTimeout(timer);\n        resolve({ ws, msg: JSON.parse(data.toString()) });\n      });\n    });\n    \n    ws.on('error', (err) => {\n      clearTimeout(timer);\n      reject(err);\n    });\n  });\n}\n```\n\nWait, but this still has the race. The `message` event could fire BETWEEN `ws.on('open')` callback starting and `ws.once('message')` being registered... but since these are synchronous operations in the same callback, the message event can't fire in between.\n\nActually, even this might not be enough if the message arrives before the open callback runs. Let me think...\n\nActually, the `ws.on('open', callback)` binds the callback to the `open` event. When `open` fires, `callback` runs. Inside `callback`, I register `ws.once('message', ...)`. The `message` event hasn't been emitted yet because it's queued after the `open` event in the event loop.\n\nSo `wsConnectAndRead` should work. The key insight is: set up the message listener INSIDE the open callback (synchronously), not after an `await wsConnect` call.\n\nBut actually, even that has a subtle issue: `ws.once('message', ...)` inside the open callback will be called multiple times if we need to read multiple messages (one for init, one for ack, etc.). The `once` means it'll only fire for the first message. For subsequent messages, we need more `ws.once('message')` calls. But those subsequent calls should be fine because they happen after the first message is received.\n\nActually, the simplest fix: set up the message listener outside the open callback, at construction time. This way, the listener is registered before any events fire:\n\n```javascript\nfunction wsConnect(url: string): Promise<WebSocket> {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n    const ws = new WebSocket(url);\n    ws.on('open', () => resolve(ws));\n    ws.on('error', reject);\n    // Don't set up message listeners here to avoid conflicts with wsRead\n  });\n}\n```\n\nThis is what I already have. The issue is the `wsRead` call after `wsConnect`. \n\nLet me use a different approach: instead of separate connect and read, combine them.","signature":"458c29fe-8830-4380-bb3e-1089cf866876"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":551,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":70144,"output_tokens":0,"service_tier":"standard