+{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"ce57149d-bbdf-4552-a258-73099e95b863","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good progress! The WebSocket init messages are now received correctly:\n- The \"receives init when connecting\" test passed\n- The \"sends an operation and receives ack\" test passed\n- The \"broadcasts operations to other clients\" test passed\n- The \"persists across server restart\" test passed\n\nThe 3 convergence tests still fail. These are the more complex tests where two clients send operations at the same version and then wait for broadcasts.\n\nThe issue is specifically with `wsRead` after the init. The `wsRead` function uses `ws.once('message', ...)`. The problem might be that the `wsRead` function is called too late, after the message was already received.\n\nWait, I see the pattern in the failing tests: they call `wsSend(ws1, ...)` and `wsSend(ws2, ...)` and then try to read. The acks and broadcasts should arrive. But maybe there's still a race where the message arrives before the listener is set up.\n\nLet me look at the \"two clients converge\" test more carefully:\n\n```javascript\nconst { ws: ws1, msg: init1 } = await wsConnectAndRead(...);\nconst { ws: ws2 } = await wsConnectAndRead(...);\n\nconst initMsg = init1 as { version: number };\n\n// Both send operations at the same version\nwsSend(ws1, {...}, version: initMsg.version);\nwsSend(ws2, {...}, version: initMsg.version);\n\n// Collect all messages\nfor (let i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n  if (i < 2) {\n    await wsRead(ws1, 3000);\n  } else {\n    await wsRead(ws2, 3000);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nBoth sends happen immediately. Then we try to read. The messages should come:\n1. ws1 gets ack (for its own insert)\n2. ws2 might get ack (for its own insert) or ws1 gets broadcast of ws2's insert\n3. ws2 gets the other message\n\nBut the order isn't deterministic. The issue might be that messages come in a different order than expected. For