wo messages? The `once` handler fires only once, so the first message triggers it, and the second message needs a new listener. But by the time we set up the second listener (during the second `wsRead` call), has the second message already been received?\n\nIn Node.js, if two WebSocket messages arrive on the same TCP packet or very close together, they could both be processed in the same event loop iteration. The `message` event would be emitted for the first, the `once` handler fires, then the `message` event for the second is emitted. Since `once` removes the listener after the first fire, the second message would have no listener. Then when we call `wsRead` again and set up a new `once` listener, the second message has already come and gone.\n\nThis is the actual bug! When two messages arrive back-to-back, the second one is lost because we're using `once`.\n\nThe fix: use `on('message', ...)` with a queue/buffer approach:\n\n```javascript\nconst messageQueue: unknown[] = [];\nws.on('message', (data) => {\n  messageQueue.push(JSON.parse(data.toString()));\n  // Resolve any pending reads...\n});\n```\n\nBut this requires a different pattern. Let me implement a buffered read approach.\n\nActually, the simplest fix: use a global message handler that queues messages, and have `wsRead` dequeue from the queue.","signature":"ce57149d-bbdf-4552-a258-73099e95b863"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":837,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":78592,"output_tokens":0,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"94645184-9d61-4647-b304-ae0c7e469be4","uuid":"5eb3c5d6-b0f9-4dfd-bb88-d23f45ddf58c"}
